<h2>Which chart</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>How much, of a few things</strong> — columns, or a ranking when the labels are long.</li>
	<li><strong>How it changed</strong> — a line; an area when the amount matters more than the slope; a sparkline when there is no room.</li>
	<li><strong>What share</strong> — a donut for three slices, a waffle for one share of a hundred, a ranking for more than five slices. A pie with eight slices is a ranking that has been folded.</li>
	<li><strong>When, across a week or a year</strong> — a heatmap or the calendar.</li>
	<li><strong>How two things relate</strong> — a scatter.</li>
	<li><strong>Exactly</strong> — a table. Every chart on these pages is also its numbers, in an <code>aria-label</code> and in the markup, and a table is the honest form of all of them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>One palette</h2>
<p>Six series colours, and they are the system’s own: the accent for the one series that matters, the ink for the one it is measured against, and then the neutral ramp. There is no second hue — a chart that needs six colours to be read is a table. Set <code>data-series="n"</code> on a series (or <code>--c</code> on one element) to pick; most charts should never go past two.</p>
{{#example}}
<ul class="im-legend im-legend-row">
	<li style="--c: var(--im-viz-1)">--im-viz-1<b>accent</b></li>
	<li style="--c: var(--im-viz-2)">--im-viz-2<b>ink</b></li>
	<li style="--c: var(--im-viz-3)">--im-viz-3<b>body</b></li>
	<li style="--c: var(--im-viz-4)">--im-viz-4<b>muted</b></li>
	<li style="--c: var(--im-viz-5)">--im-viz-5<b>faint</b></li>
	<li style="--c: var(--im-viz-6)">--im-viz-6<b>surface</b></li>
</ul>
{{/example}}
<p>A sequential scale — a heatmap, a calendar — is the one colour mixed into the track by the value, never past 85% of it, so <em>more</em> is always <em>more of the accent</em> and nothing is a wall. A chart never names a hue of its own: <code>--im-chart-color</code> is series one, and that is the only knob a page should need. The quiet idiom, used all through these pages, is <strong>one thing in the accent and the rest in grey</strong>: <code>data-on</code> on the column, the leaf or the bar to notice.</p>

<h2>Arriving</h2>
<p>A chart arrives once, when it scrolls into view, and then never moves again. Put <code>data-im-reveal="none"</code> on it (<code>im-motion.js</code> marks it revealed; <code>im-charts.js</code> numbers its children as <code>--n</code>) and each kind does the one thing that says what it is:</p>
<div class="im-table-scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Chart</th><th>Arrives by</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Columns, bars, a ranking, a bullet, a bar in a table cell</td><td>Growing from the baseline or the left, one after another (<code>--im-viz-step</code>, 40ms apart).</td></tr>
<tr><td>A line, an area, a step</td><td>Wiped on from the left; the last point lands as the line reaches it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A scatter</td><td>The points pop in, in order.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A donut, a gauge, a waffle</td><td>Sweeping round from the top (<code>--im-viz-sweep</code>, 0 → 1).</td></tr>
<tr><td>A heatmap, a calendar, a matrix</td><td>Cell by cell, in reading order.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A treemap, a funnel</td><td>The leaves and the stages rise in turn.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A stat</td><td>Counts up to its figure (<code>data-im-count</code>).</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Every one reads the motion tokens — <code>--im-duration-slow</code> for a fill, <code>-long</code> for a sweep or a line, <code>--im-ease-out</code> for anything arriving — and every one stops under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, where the chart is simply there. Nothing on these pages loops: a hover readout answers in <code>--im-duration-fast</code> and that is all the motion a chart has after it has arrived.</p>

<h2>Numbers</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Geist Mono, tabular figures</strong>, everywhere a number sits in a column or ticks an axis — so digits line up and a counting figure does not jitter.</li>
	<li><strong>Short where the axis is short</strong>: <code>12k</code>, <code>1.2M</code>, never <code>12,480</code> on a tick. The script does this for a line’s axis; a page does it by hand for columns.</li>
	<li><strong>The axis starts at zero</strong> for anything that is an amount. A line that measures a rate may start where the data starts, and says so in its note.</li>
	<li><strong>A value is a share</strong>: every bar, cell and slice reads <code>--v</code> from 0 to 1, scaled by the page against the top of its own axis. The chart does no arithmetic it would have to guess at.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Honest limits</h2>
<ul>
	<li>No zoom, no brushing, no live data. For something a reader will <em>study</em>, embed a charting library and style it with the same tokens (<code>--im-viz-1…6</code>, <code>--im-viz-line</code>, <code>--im-font-mono</code>) so it belongs.</li>
	<li>Every chart carries <code>role="img"</code> and an <code>aria-label</code> that <strong>says the numbers</strong>; the points of a line are buttons a keyboard can reach, and each one speaks its value.</li>
	<li>Where a reader needs the figures, put the table beside the chart. It is on <a href="/data/numbers/">Numbers &amp; tables</a>.</li>
</ul>
